---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import BusinessPerspectiveNote from '../../components/BusinessPerspectiveNote.astro';
import ToolNextStep from '../../components/ToolNextStep.astro';
import CourseCostPlanner from '../../components/CourseCostPlanner.astro';

const title = 'Course Cost Planner: Full Cost of a Course in India';
const description = 'Add tuition, living cost and one-time costs to see the full price of a course, the money still missing and what to do next. Free, private.';
const canonical = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/tools/course-cost-planner/';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Nav activePage="tools" />
  <BusinessPerspectiveNote />
  <main>
    <section class="tool-hero container"><p class="eyebrow">FREE PLANNING CALCULATOR</p><h1>Course Cost <span>Planner</span></h1><p class="lead">See the full cost of a course before you pay a deposit, not only the tuition number in the brochure. You get a total, the money still missing, and three next steps you can copy to WhatsApp.</p><p class="tool-limits">It works only from the numbers you type, so it is not a fee quote. To check a loan, use the <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/tools/education-loan-emi-calculator/">education loan EMI calculator</a>.</p></section>
    <section class="container tool-shell"><CourseCostPlanner /></section>
    <section class="container tool-explainer"><h2>Use it before paying</h2><div class="explainer-grid"><article><h3>Collect the real inputs</h3><p>Use the current fee notice. Ask about hostel, transport, exams, equipment, refund rules and compulsory extras.</p></article><article><h3>Keep uncertain money separate</h3><p>Do not count a scholarship you have not received, a future salary or hoped-for part-time income as funding.</p></article><article><h3>Compare the route</h3><p>Run the same numbers for a lower-cost course and write down what proof would make the costlier one worth it.</p></article></div>
    <h2>A worked example (an illustration, not a real fee)</h2><p>A family looks at a 3-year degree. Tuition and compulsory fees are ₹2,10,000, living cost is ₹7,000 a month (₹2,52,000 over 36 months) and a laptop and deposit add ₹25,000. The total is ₹4,87,000, or about ₹1,62,333 a year.</p><p>They already hold ₹60,000, so ₹4,27,000 is still to arrange, which is about ₹11,861 a month over the course. If the family can set aside ₹8,000 a month, ₹3,861 a month is still missing. They would look at a cheaper college, a scholarship they can confirm, or a loan whose EMI they have checked.</p>
    <h2>When to talk to a person</h2><p>Ask the college accounts office for the payment schedule and refund rule in writing. For a loan, ask the bank officer for the offer letter before you sign anything.</p><p>Planning the whole family budget? Read <a href="/blog/parents/how-much-to-spend-on-child-education-india/">how much to spend on a child's education in India</a>.</p></section>
    <ToolNextStep toolName="Course Cost Planner" audience="parent" />
  </main><Footer />
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .tool-hero{padding:5rem 1.25rem 2rem}.tool-hero h1{max-width:850px}.tool-hero h1 span{color:var(--gold,#e5b84a)}.lead{max-width:760px;font-size:1.15rem;line-height:1.7}.tool-limits{max-width:760px;font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.6;color:var(--muted,#b9c2c1)}.tool-shell{max-width:1050px}.tool-explainer{padding:2rem 1.25rem 5rem}.tool-explainer h2{margin-top:0}.explainer-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:1rem}.explainer-grid article{padding:1rem;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14);border-radius:14px}.explainer-grid h3{margin-top:0}@media(max-width:720px){.tool-hero{padding-top:3rem}.explainer-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style>
